WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Uncertainty means incomplete knowledge about facts, future states, risks, or outcomes.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.310
It is not a personal failure.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:10.245
It is the normal condition under which most important decisions are made.

00:00:10.245 --> 00:00:12.100
Technical teams do not know every dependency.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.260
Leaders do not know every market change.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.580
Security teams do not know every attacker path.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.730
Educators do not know exactly how every learner will respond.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.515
Probability is a practical language for uncertainty.

00:00:21.515 --> 00:00:25.515
It lets people move beyond vague statements like maybe, probably, or unlikely.

00:00:25.515 --> 00:00:27.970
A forecast does not need to be perfect to be useful.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:31.320
Saying there is a thirty percent chance of a delay is different from saying delay is

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:31.920
possible.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.960
The first statement can support planning, contingency, and comparison.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.195
The second may be too vague to guide action.

00:00:37.195 --> 00:00:39.500
Base rates are one of the simplest ways to improve judgment.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.075
A base rate is background information about how often something tends to happen in similar

00:00:44.075 --> 00:00:44.900
situations.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.100
Before assuming a project is unique, ask how often similar projects finish on time.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.270
Before assuming a control will be easy to deploy, ask how similar deployments have gone.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:54.460
Specific evidence matters,

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.315
but base rates keep teams from treating every case as entirely special.

00:00:58.315 --> 00:00:59.805
Confidence ranges are also useful.

00:00:59.805 --> 00:01:02.550
Instead of pretending a cost estimate is a single precise number,

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.045
a team might say the likely range is between two values,

00:01:05.045 --> 00:01:07.620
with major uncertainty around integration work.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.531
Scenario thinking helps by asking what the world looks like if assumptions are wrong.

00:01:11.531 --> 00:01:13.060
Bayesian updating, at a high level,

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:17.010
means revising beliefs as new evidence arrives instead of defending the first forecast

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:17.580
forever.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.140
The phrase I do not know should not end the analysis.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:21.660
It should begin better analysis.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:22.660
What do we not know?

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:23.935
Which unknowns matter most?

00:01:23.935 --> 00:01:25.660
What evidence could reduce uncertainty?

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.220
Which assumptions should be monitored?

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.620
The goal is not omniscience.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.160
The goal is to make uncertainty explicit enough that decisions can be more resilient.
